1. Design Philosophy: How Apple Decisions Are Actually Made

Every native Apple interface is governed by two overlapping triads—the classic
interaction foundation (1987 / iOS 7) and the modern Liquid Glass spatial era
(WWDC25+):

The Core Triads

1.  Classic Interaction Foundation (Clarity • Deference • Depth)
      - Clarity: Every pixel must earn its place. Text is legible at every
        distance, icons are immediately unambiguous, and decoration never
        obscures function.
      - Deference: The UI must never compete with the user's content. Chrome
        shrinks, goes translucent, hides during scrolling/immersion, and lets
        full-bleed photos, maps, or documents own the viewport.
      - Depth: Visual layers, distinct z-planes, lighting, and realistic
        physical motion tell the user where they came from, what floats above
        what, and what happens if they let go.
2.  Modern Era Triad (Hierarchy • Harmony • Consistency)
      - Hierarchy: Establish strict separation between the floating functional
        control layer (bars, pills, sheets) and the content layer scrolling
        underneath. Never style content cards with the same floating glass
        material used for navigation chrome.
      - Harmony: Software geometry, corner curvature, color temperature, and
        motion echo the physical hardware bezel and room environment (concentric
        corners, True Tone, adaptive luma).
      - Consistency: Design one conceptual anatomy per feature and express it
        idiomatically across devices—never copy-paste an iPhone tab bar onto
        macOS or a desktop menu onto watchOS.

The Hidden 1987 Axioms (Still Enforced Internally)

  - Direct Manipulation: Users act directly on objects (pinch the photo, drag
    the sheet, pull the list), not via proxy forms or tiny +/- arrows.
  - Forgiveness Over Nagging: Prefer instant execution + Undo over confirmation
    popups. Modal interruptions should feel rare and serious.
  - Perceived Stability: Never dynamically hide or remove primary navigation
    tabs when empty—keep tabs stable and explain empty state inside the view.

2. Spatial Layout, Ergonomic Grids & Optical Alignment

A. Layout Margins, Grids & Readable Width Caps

  - Spacing Unit System: Strict 8pt macro grid (8 / 16 / 24 / 32pt) with 4pt
    micro steps (4 / 12pt) for tight icon-to-label pairing.
  - Container Side Margins:
      - Compact Width (iPhone portrait): 16pt standard (20pt on Pro Max / roomy
        editorial layouts).
      - Regular Width (iPad / large panes): 20pt standard; 48pt for full-width
        containers \ge 768\text{pt}.
  - Readable Width Ceiling (readableContentGuide): Even on a 13" iPad or 32" Mac
    monitor, never stretch paragraphs across the entire screen. Cap body reading
    columns at 672pt max width (centered inside margins; scales dynamically
    upward/downward as Dynamic Type changes so line length stays within
    comfortable ~50–75 characters per eye sweep).
  - Edge-to-Edge Content & backgroundExtensionEffect(): Background media, lists,
    and hero images always bleed edge-to-edge under bars and sidebars. If a
    sidebar covers an important part of a hero image, Apple mirrors & blurs the
    adjacent edge underneath the sidebar (backgroundExtensionEffect) rather than
    cropping or leaving dead opaque sidebars.

B. Ergonomic Interactive Target Floors (Per Modality)

| Input Modality                | Minimum Interactive Hit Box                                                                           | Inter-Element Gap               | Visual Size Rule                                                                                            |
| :---------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :------------------------------ | :---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Touch (iOS / iPadOS)**      | **`44 × 44 pt`**                                                                                      | `>= 8 pt` (`12–16pt` preferred) | Glyph may be `16–22pt`, **hit frame must be padded to `>= 44×44pt`**.                                       |
| **Gaze + Pinch (visionOS)**   | **`60 × 60 pt`**                                                                                      | **`>= 12–16 pt`**               | Visual button can be `28–44pt`, padded with `8pt+` invisible hit halo to absorb natural eye micro-saccades. |
| **Precision Pointer (macOS)** | `20–24 pt` (compact), `28–32 pt` (standard)                                                           | `>= 6–8 pt`                     | Hover cursor highlights/lifts target cleanly.                                                               |
| **Thumb Ergonomics (iPhone)** | Primary actions in **bottom 40%**; top bar reserved for passive titles, Back, and infrequent actions. | —                               | Bottom-anchored floating bars (`Search`, `Compose`, `Tab Bar`) keep one-handed reach effortless.            |

C. Optical Alignment vs. Mathematical Centering

Software built strictly by bounding-box math always looks subtly crooked. Apple
corrects optical centers:

  - Asymmetric Triangle / Arrow (▶ Play icon inside circle): Geometric
    bounding-box centering makes a play triangle look shifted left because most
    visual mass sits on its vertical left edge. Shift triangular icons +6% to
    +8% of container radius (+1.5pt to +2.5pt) right toward their center of
    mass.
  - Optical Baseline for Symbols + Text: Never center an SF Symbol by its outer
    image frame next to Text; align to the cap-height midpoint / first-baseline
    so symbols sit naturally on the same visual horizon as uppercase glyphs.
  - Left-Align Critical Text: In iOS 26+ HIG, prefer left-aligned titles,
    headers, and list hierarchy inside complex cards/bars rather than weak
    center-alignment unless the card is a single minimal hero status.

3. Interaction Choreography: "Fluid Interfaces" (WWDC18 803+)

Apple’s interaction philosophy (Chan Karunamuni, Designing Fluid Interfaces)
rejects traditional "user acts \to wait for movie to play" choreography:

"Fluidity is responsiveness and redirectability, not frame rate."

The 5 Non-Negotiable Fluid Interaction Laws

1.  Zero-Latency Acknowledgment (0ms Response): Every touch down must
    immediately acknowledge contact in Frame 1 (subtle scale press, luminance
    shift, finger glow). If an object doesn't visibly react until finger-up, it
    feels dead/laggy.
2.  Continuous Redirection & Interruption: A user must be able to grab, pause,
    reverse, or throw any sheet, drawer, navigation transition, or zoom card
    mid-flight. Never lock input during transitions (isUserInteractionEnabled =
    false is a severe UX smell) except during irreversible disk/payment commits.
3.  Momentum Matches Intent (v_0 Conservation): Fast flicks skip intermediate
    detents or fly across tabs; slow drags settle gently. Velocity from gesture
    release passes uninterrupted into the ODE spring.
4.  Asymmetric Entrances vs. Exits: Elements exit faster than they enter.
    Entering UI guides spatial orientation; dismissing UI gets out of the user's
    way immediately.
5.  Spatial Continuity (matchedTransitionSource / Hero Morph): When opening an
    item (photo, note, card, album), morph the tapped card directly into the
    detail canvas (.navigationTransition(.zoom)) rather than cross-fading or
    sliding in a disconnected sheet from below. Keep shared elements (image,
    title) continuously alive across frames.

Choreography & State-Transition Rules

  - Never Teleport Values (Numbers & Labels): Numeric counters, timers,
    currencies, and status text roll or cross-dissolve smoothly
    (.contentTransition(.numericText())), never snap harshly.
  - Tight Micro-Stagger Budget: If staggering grid cards or list rows on appear,
    keep stagger delay to 15–30ms per visible row and cap total reveal cascade
    at <= 180ms (skip animation completely for off-screen rows).
  - Ban Generic .easeInOut: Use critically damped or softly underdamped springs
    for position/scale, linear/fast-out-ease for opacity fades, and never use
    sluggish symmetrical CSS ease-in-out on user-triggered gestures.

4. Navigation Architecture & Modality Discipline

A. Choose Exactly One Primary Structural Model

1.  Flat Destinations (TabBar / Adaptive Sidebar):
      - 3–5 peer top-level sections (avoid creating a "More" overflow tab on
        iPhone).
      - Golden Rule: Tabs are destinations, never actions (don't put a "+"
        Create button acting as a fake tab inside the tab bar unless it's a
        clearly separated detached action or toolbar button).
      - Every tab preserves its own independent navigation stack and scroll
        position. Tapping the active tab again pops to root / scrolls to top.
      - Never hide the tab bar inside normal navigation pushes unless entering a
        dedicated fullscreen media viewer or modal task.
2.  Hierarchical Drill-Down (NavigationStack):
      - Large Title (34pt bold) collapses smoothly on scroll into inline bar
        (17pt semibold).
      - Back always resolves to the hierarchical parent (never blindly "last
        visited URL").
      - Never put custom opaque backgrounds or hard 1px rules under navigation
        bars—content flows beneath transparent/glass chrome.
3.  Content-First Canvas (Maps, Notes Canvas, Media Editors):
      - Edge-to-edge live workspace; floating top/bottom pill toolbars +
        optional trailing Inspector.

B. Modality & Presentation Escalation Ladder

Only interrupt flow when a focused subtask or critical choice demands isolation.
Always choose the lowest-disruption container that solves the task:

| Level | Pattern                          | When to Use                                                                                      | Strict Rules                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| :---- | :------------------------------- | :----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **1** | **Inline Expansion / Inspector** | Inspecting/editing properties without losing main canvas context (trailing sidebar on iPad/Mac). | Changes apply live immediately; no "Save/Cancel" modal needed.                                                                                                                                    |
| **2** | **Popover**                      | Quick single-purpose properties anchored directly to a tapped button on iPad/Mac.                | Auto-dismisses on outside tap; keep focused to 1–few related controls.                                                                                                                            |
| **3** | **Sheet (with Detents)**         | Focused multi-step task (Compose, Filter, Settings).                                             | Use `.medium` / `.large` detents + grabber; **only one sheet stacked at a time**; dirty unsaved changes intercept swipe-dismiss with a bottom Action Sheet (`Save` / `Discard` / `Keep Editing`). |
| **4** | **Full-Screen Cover**            | Immersive capture, camera, playback, full canvas editing.                                        | Must provide unmistakable explicit exit (`×` or `Done`).                                                                                                                                          |
| **5** | **Alert**                        | **Emergency / Irreversible Danger ONLY**.                                                        | **Never** show on app launch; **never** show for routine info or undoable deletions; max 2 buttons.                                                                                               |

5. Iconography: SF Symbols & Layered App Icons (Icon Composer)

A. SF Symbols System Rules (7,000+ Vector Glyphs)

  - Prefer System Symbols Over Custom Icons for all standard concepts (share,
    trash, search, plus, xmark)—zero cognitive friction and automatic Dynamic
    Type + optical weight matching.
  - 4 Rendering Modes (Pick by Context):
      - Monochrome: Single flat color—dense text-inline badges.
      - Hierarchical: One color, automatic layer opacity depth across
        primary/secondary/tertiary paths—ideal default for toolbars & Liquid
        Glass controls.
      - Palette: 2–3 explicit contrasting colors assigned per layer.
      - Multicolor: Apple's intrinsic semantic colors (e.g., green badge, yellow
        sun, red trash)—best in rich list rows and status indicators.
  - Continuous Interpolation Engine (Custom Symbols): Author 3 source drawings
    (Ultralight-Small, Regular-Small, Black-Small) with identical point/path
    topology—the system interpolates all 27 weight × scale combinations
    seamlessly. Center visually on the cap-height midpoint.
  - Semantic Animation Vocabulary:
      - Bounce: Discrete tap acknowledgment.
      - Pulse / Breathe: Indefinite active/connecting/recording state (must have
        explicit stop condition).
      - Wiggle / Rotate: Layer-aware mechanical motion (fan blades spin inside
        stationary housing; alarm bell shakes inside frame).
      - Replace / Magic Replace: Morphs shared enclosures across state flips
        (e.g., mic \leftrightarrow mic.slash) using directional Draw Off / Draw
        On stroke animation.
      - Variable Value vs. Variable Draw: Layer thresholds (0.0–1.0, where only
        0% is empty) vs. smooth progressive stroke length (SF Symbols 7).

B. Layered Liquid Glass App Icons (Icon Composer .icon)

  - Never Pre-Mask or Pre-Shade: Export 1024 × 1024 full-bleed opaque background
    + separate vector SVG foreground layers inside Apple's Icon Composer. Do not
    bake rounded corners, fake static specular glare, or drop shadows—the OS
    masks the concentric squircle and applies live P3 glass refraction, specular
    rim highlights, and parallax per layer across Default (Light), Dark, Clear,
    and Tinted Home Screen appearances.
  - Three Survival Tests:
    1.  Recognition: Instant identification without reading the app name
        underneath.
    2.  Scale Survival: Crisp from 1024px App Store hero down to 29pt Settings
        list row—zero unreadable micro-text.
    3.  Grid Separation: Distinct foreground silhouette and luminance contrast
        even when the user enables system-wide Monochrome Tinted mode.

6. Sound Design & Audio-Haptic Philosophy (WWDC19 + Hugo Verweij)

Visuals alone feel weightless without acoustic/tactile grounding:

The 3 Acoustic Pillars

1.  Causality: Sound is never ambient wallpaper; it happens strictly as the
    physical consequence of an object touching, locking, snapping, completing,
    or failing.
2.  Harmony: All UI sounds in an app share a coherent harmonic family and
    physical material metaphor (warm wood, tuned glass, muted felt/marimba
    transients)—never jarring sine-wave beeps or clashing third-party SFX packs.
3.  Utility: Sound communicates confirmation, error, or off-screen completion
    without forcing the user to look down.

The 4 Acoustic Engineering Rules

  - The "1st vs. 100th Use" Test: A dramatic laser whoosh that feels cool on
    onboarding #1 becomes unbearable annoyance by action #100. Keep routine UI
    audio ultra-short (30–120ms), clean-tailed, and un-fatiguing.
  - Earshot Calibration: Sonic amplitude must scale proportionally with visual
    importance; micro-interactions whisper softly.
  - Hardware Mute Respect: Respect the physical Silent Switch / Focus modes
    using .ambient audio session categories for non-essential UI feedback.
  - Sync Latency Budgets: Human audio–visual perception tolerates only +45ms
    early to -125ms late (ITU-R BT.1359), and haptic+audio physical coincidence
    requires < 10ms lock to the exact visual spring detent/contact frame
    (CHHapticPattern bundling audio + transient haptic inside one .ahap
    timeline).

7. Microcopy, Writing Rules & Perceived Performance

A. Apple UI Writing Grammar

  - Capitalization Split (HIG / Apple Style Guide):
      - Title Style (Capitalize main words; lowercase short
        articles/conjunctions/prepositions \le 3 letters): Navigation titles,
        Tab labels, Button titles ("Add to Favorites"), Menu items, Section
        headers—no ending punctuation.
      - Sentence Style (First word + proper nouns only): Alert titles ("Delete
        this draft?"), Alert body messages, List descriptions, Input
        placeholders, Accessibility labels, Toggles/Checkboxes.
  - Consequence Verbs on Buttons: Never use "Yes" / "No" / "OK" on decision
    dialogs. Name the exact consequence in 1–2 words: "Delete Photo" vs.
    "Cancel" (and style destructive actions with .destructive red role; safe
    non-destructive choice as default).
  - Zero Blame / Zero Robotic Jargon: Never say "You entered an invalid email"
    or "Error 0x4F2"; avoid patronizing pronouns (you, your, my) in system
    alerts. State the situation plainly + give the concrete next action in 1–2
    short lines.

B. Perceived Latency & Waiting Rules

  - 0–100 ms: Show no loading indicator—instantaneous transition.
  - 100 ms – 1.0 s: Show measured skeletons (placeholder blocks calculated
    directly from real font line-heights/metrics and rounded to match real card
    shapes, driven by one synchronized global shimmer clock). Never flash a
    spinning wheel for < 1s—Stanford/Apple HCI shows frantic spinners actively
    amplify perceived wait anxiety.
  - > 1.0 s: Show determinate progress (0% -> 100%) whenever possible, render
    cached/partial content immediately, and keep the rest of the interface
    interactive (never block the whole screen with a modal spinner).

8. Spatial Computing Ergonomics (visionOS Design Grammar)

In 3D space, Z stops being a drop-shadow illusion and becomes physical distance:

1.  Ergonomic Comfort Zone (1–2 m & Central 30° Cone):
      - Place primary windows at 1.0 – 2.0 meters (< 1m for direct arm's-length
        touch; > 3m strictly for ambient environmental objects).
      - Keep active UI inside the central 30° forward field-of-view cone to
        prevent neck strain. When needing more canvas area, expand horizontally,
        never tall vertically.
2.  Vergence–Accommodation Safety: Never lunge UI rapidly toward the user's nose
    or place crisp tiny text simultaneously across wildly conflicting focal
    planes.
3.  Eyes as Pointer + Indirect Pinch:
      - Users look at a target (60×60pt, >= 12pt gap), receive immediate
        hoverEffect luminance feedback on the hovered element before clicking,
        and pinch anywhere naturally in their lap.
      - Never head-lock HUD overlays directly onto the user's face—provide
        world/body-trailing anchors (accessibilityPrefersHeadAnchorAlternative).
4.  Adaptive Glass Windows: Because rooms vary from sunlit patios to dark
    bedrooms, visionOS glass adjusts tint/vibrancy automatically against real
    camera passthrough lighting; bold/slightly thicker SF Pro weights + white
    typography + Vibrancy hierarchy replace heavy black text and light/dark
    theme toggles.

9. Accessibility as Core Architecture (The 6 Deterministic Fallbacks)

Apple treats accessibility as first-class parallel rendering paths, not post-hoc
labels:

1.  VoiceOver Semantic Tree: Group composite cards
    (accessibilityElement(children: .combine)) so a user swipes once per card
    instead of 5 times across icon, title, subtitle, chevron; strip decorative
    shapes; provide custom rotor/swipe actions for hidden gestures.
2.  Dynamic Type (xS \to AX5 Reflow): Every text element uses semantic styles
    (.body, .headline, .caption). When dynamicTypeSize.isAccessibilitySize
    (AX1–AX5) activates, automatically switch horizontal HStack layouts into
    stacked VStack layouts and remove fixed row heights so nothing ever clips or
    truncates (...).
3.  Reduce Motion (accessibilityReduceMotion / PrefersCrossFadeTransitions):
    Strip spring overshoots, hero zoom flights, parallax tilts, and animated
    shader noise/mesh motion—replace cleanly with 0.2–0.25s opacity cross-fades.
4.  Reduce Transparency & Increase Contrast (accessibilityReduceTransparency):
    Replace translucent glass/blurs with opaque elevated surfaces (>= 4.5:1 WCAG
    AA text contrast, >= 3:1 UI controls) and crisp 1.0pt solid borders.
5.  Differentiate Without Color (accessibilityDifferentiateWithoutColor): Never
    indicate errors, online states, or trends purely by Red vs. Green
    dots—always pair color with distinct SF Symbol glyphs, labels, or geometry.
6.  Large Content Viewer (accessibilityShowsLargeContentViewer): Fixed-height
    bars (tab bar, bottom toolbar) that cannot grow to 53pt text must show an
    enlarged centered HUD preview on long-press.

